#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153267} :activity/id #uuid "32095b5a-ea4a-5f14-a569-3cb501bb8677" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050150 :topic/id #uuid "3185fbd2-cc78-5eb1-ba66-6a65b74ad8a2" :topic/math-academy-id 2547} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153267 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153330 :tutorial/content "Suppose $\\{ f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\}$ is a set of functions defined on an interval $I\\subseteq R.$\n\n- The set is called **linearly dependent** on $I$ if there exist real numbers $k_1, k_2, \\ldots, k_n,$ *not all zero*, such that\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I.$ \n\nIntuitively, at least one of the functions can be written as a linear combination of the others.\n\n- The set is called **linearly independent** on $I$ if the *only* way the linear combination\n\n$$\nk_1 f_1(x) + k_2 f_2(x) + \\cdots + k_n f_n(x)\n$$\n\ncan equal $0$ for all $x \\in I$ is by taking\n\n$$\nk_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that these definitions closely parallel the notions of linear dependence and independence for vectors in a vector space.\n\nFor example, let's consider the set of functions \n\n$$\n\\{ \\sin x, \\, \\cos x, \\, 2\\sin x \\}\n$$\n\ndefined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nNotice that for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R},$ we have that\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot \\sin x +\n0 \\cdot \\cos x +\n(-1) \\cdot 2\\sin x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since not all the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly dependent.\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "4d8e0478-c6f1-57b4-b41b-1fe464cb7fdc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14499 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3e3b73d3-8c02-5744-a1ae-6c50e8804ed2" :step/math-academy-id 593388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153268}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153268 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152277 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151908 :question/id #uuid "708d2d6d-df17-57e3-bff2-ee8809cf9e3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21683"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe4c8bb4-b7c0-5afc-86fd-40c20df9b953" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048257}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152565} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308544}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Dependent and Linearly Independent Functions"} :step/id #uuid "be6dde9b-9d0b-585b-a6b3-25eff080cc7b" :step/math-academy-id 593389 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153269}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153269 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153328 :tutorial/content "Suppose $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are differentiable functions defined on some interval $I.$ Then, the **Wronskian** is defined as \n\n$$\n|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'} & {y}_{2}^{'}\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nand it has the following important properties:\n\n> If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I,$ then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n\n> If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\n**Watch out!** We cannot deduce any information from $W = 0$ in cases where $y_1$ and $y_2$ are not known to be solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation." :tutorial/id #uuid "ede0bd1e-1d97-5029-bbb3-a95bbadc7c88" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14497 :tutorial/title "The Wronskian"} :step/id #uuid "77116319-3453-5eec-ac47-013a9a68819f" :step/math-academy-id 593392 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153270}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153270 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152278 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151905 :question/id #uuid "3da98f86-afab-5b4c-b51a-2ebb42248da1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21680"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050677}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348310}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"} :step/id #uuid "ba9f7dc5-2fb4-5f3b-8a80-2aa8c6b90d55" :step/math-academy-id 593393 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153271}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153271 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152279 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151906 :question/id #uuid "7e72c2e1-8bbd-5047-9394-5df205a9c85a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21681"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308561} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308562} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308563} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391420}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"} :step/id #uuid "25bf33fb-6ffa-53ff-952f-c30347b7e55c" :step/math-academy-id 593397 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153272}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153272 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153329 :tutorial/content "A **fundamental set of solutions** to a homogeneous, linear differential equation of degree $n$ is a set of precisely $n$ linearly independent solutions.\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''(x) - 4y(x) = 0.\n$$\n\nUsing direct substitution, it's straightforward to show that $y_1(x) = e^{2x}$ and $y_2(x) = e^{-2x}$ are the solutions to the equation. \n\nLet's keep track of our solutions by putting them into a set $S,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\left\\{e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the Wronskian of our solutions, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{'} & {y}_{2}^{'}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{'}-{y}_{1}^{'}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{2x}){({e}^{-2x})}^{'}-{({e}^{2x})}^{'}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{2x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2-2 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not zero, the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent. \n\nBecause the original equation is of degree two, and since the set $S$ contains *precisely* two linearly independent functions, we say that the set $S$ is a fundamental set of solutions to the differential equation.\n\n**Important:** The fundamental set of solutions is not unique. For example, the set $S',$ given by\n\n$$\nS' = \\left\\{4e^{2x}, e^{-2x}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis also a fundamental set of solutions. \n\nAs a general rule, we tend to pick the simplest set of linearly independent functions when constructing our fundamental set of solutions." :tutorial/id #uuid "e1733749-f7ac-5c62-80aa-f971e91c66d0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14498 :tutorial/title "Sets of Fundamental Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "d45e0175-eb26-56f3-9d0a-ff5fb71127aa" :step/math-academy-id 593400 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153273}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153273 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152280 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151907 :question/id #uuid "c330ca05-3b63-5525-b51e-24d0926a359c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21682"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308590}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "817b1fa5-bf11-5056-811f-2154d660e047" :step/math-academy-id 593401}] :activity/title "Linear Independence of Solutions to Homogeneous ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152305}]}
